use std
into meshopt.amni

Namespace meshopt; imports math. Decodes buffer views compressed with the glTF extensions EXT_meshopt_compression and KHR_meshopt_compression (vertex stream versions 0 and 1, triangle and index streams, and the octahedral, quaternion, exponential and color filters), written to match the meshoptimizer reference decoder byte for byte. Nothing here may crash or read past the end of a damaged stream: every read is checked against the stream's length, and a stream that is wrong in any way makes the decoder give false.

Constants: `MODE_ATTRIBUTES = 0`, `MODE_TRIANGLES = 1`, `MODE_INDICES = 2`, `FILTER_NONE = 0`, `FILTER_OCTAHEDRAL = 1`, `FILTER_QUATERNION = 2`, `FILTER_EXPONENTIAL = 3`, `FILTER_COLOR = 4`.

`mode_of` takes a name and gives 0 for "ATTRIBUTES", 1 for "TRIANGLES", 2 for "INDICES" and -1 for anything else. `filter_of` takes a name and gives 0 for an empty name or "NONE", 1 for "OCTAHEDRAL", 2 for "QUATERNION", 3 for "EXPONENTIAL", 4 for "COLOR" and -1 for anything else.

`unzig` of a whole number v undoes zigzag coding: when the lowest bit of v is set it is minus one minus (v shifted right by one), otherwise v shifted right by one.

`varint` takes a stream and a position and reads an unsigned LEB128 number starting there: each byte adds its low 7 bits, the first byte's bits lowest, and a byte below 128 ends the number. It gives two whole numbers, the number and the position just after it; when the bytes run out before a byte below 128, or after 5 bytes, the position it gives is -1.

`put_index` appends the low 32 bits of a whole number to an output byte list little-endian, as 2 bytes when the given size is 2 and as 4 bytes otherwise.

`attributes` takes a stream, a count, a stride and an output byte list, decodes mode 0, appends count times stride bytes to the output and gives true when it worked; on any failure it gives false and the output keeps no new bytes. It fails when stride is not a multiple of 4, is below 4 or above 256, when count is below zero, or when the stream is empty. The first byte gives the version: 0xa0 is version 0 and 0xa1 is version 1; any other first byte fails. The tail size is stride for version 0 and stride plus stride over 4 for version 1, and the padded tail size is the larger of the tail size and 32 for version 0 or 24 for version 1; the stream must hold at least 1 plus the padded tail size bytes. The baseline element is the stride bytes starting at the stream's length minus the tail size; for version 1 the channel bytes are the stride over 4 bytes right after it. The previous element starts as a copy of the baseline. The largest block holds the smaller of 256 and (8192 integer-divided by stride, rounded down to a multiple of 16) elements. The reader starts at position 1, and before every read it checks that the bytes it needs end no later than the stream's length minus the padded tail size, failing otherwise.

Elements are decoded in blocks of the smaller of the elements still to decode and the largest block. A block of n elements has a group count of n divided by 16 rounded up and a header byte count of the group count divided by 4 rounded up. For version 1 the block starts with stride over 4 control bytes, and byte position k's control is the two bits at bit 2 times (k mod 4) of control byte k integer-divided by 4; for version 0 every control is 0. A delta table holds stride times n raw delta bytes, all zero at the start of the block. Then for each byte position k from 0 to stride minus 1, in order: control 2 stores nothing (its deltas stay zero); control 3 stores n raw bytes, which are its deltas for elements 0 to n minus 1; control 0 or 1 stores the header bytes and then the groups in order, where group g's mode is the two bits at bit 2 times (g mod 4) of header byte g integer-divided by 4. The mode gives a bit width: for version 0 the modes 0, 1, 2, 3 give 0, 2, 4, 8 bits; for version 1 with control 0 they give 0, 1, 2, 4 bits and with control 1 they give 1, 2, 4, 8 bits. A group's 16 deltas: width 0 is all zero and stores no bytes; width 1 stores 2 bytes where delta m is bit (m mod 8) of byte m integer-divided by 8, the lowest bit first; width 2 stores 4 bytes where delta m is the two bits at bit 6 minus 2 times (m mod 4) of byte m integer-divided by 4; width 4 stores 8 bytes where delta m is the high nibble of byte m integer-divided by 2 for even m and the low nibble for odd m; width 8 stores 16 bytes, one delta each. For widths 1, 2 and 4, a delta with all its bits set (1, 3 or 15) is a sentinel: it is replaced by the next byte read after the group's packed bytes, in order of m. Delta m of group g is element 16 times g plus m of byte position k, kept only when that element is below n.

Then each element i of the block, in order, is rebuilt from the previous element four bytes at a time. For byte group j (bytes 4 times j to 4 times j plus 3) the channel is 0 for version 0 and for version 1 the low two bits of channel byte j; channel 3 fails. Channel 0: each of the four bytes becomes the previous element's byte plus `unzig` of its delta, kept to 8 bits. Channel 1: for each of the two 16-bit halves, the delta is the 16-bit little-endian number made of the two bytes' deltas, the value is the previous element's 16-bit little-endian value plus `unzig` of that delta, kept to 16 bits. Channel 2: the delta is the 32-bit little-endian number made of the four bytes' deltas, rotated right by r bits within 32 bits, where r is the channel byte shifted right by 4, and the value is the previous element's 32-bit little-endian value bitwise-xor that rotated delta. The rebuilt bytes become the previous element and go to the output at the element's index times stride. After the last block the reader must stand exactly at the stream's length minus the padded tail size, or it fails.

`triangles` takes a stream, a count, an index size and an output byte list, decodes mode 1 and appends count indices of the given size each (via `put_index`), giving true when it worked; on any failure it gives false and the output keeps no new bytes. It fails when count is below zero or not a multiple of 3, size is not 2 or 4, the stream is shorter than 17 plus count divided by 3 bytes, or its first byte is not 0xe1. The codes are the count divided by 3 bytes after the first byte, one per triangle; the extra data starts right after the codes; the code table `aux` is the last 16 bytes of the stream. The state is `next` 0, `last` 0, an edge FIFO of 16 pairs of whole numbers, a vertex FIFO of 16 whole numbers (all zero at the start), and a write position for each FIFO starting at 0; pushing writes at the write position and moves it on by one modulo 16, and reading FIFO entry n gives the entry at the write position minus 1 minus n modulo 16, so entry 0 is the newest. Reading an index means reading a `varint` from the extra data (false when that fails or reaches into the code table), turning it into a delta with `unzig` of the number, adding the delta to `last`, keeping `last` to 32 bits as an unsigned number, and taking `last`. For each code byte with high nibble X and low nibble Y: when X is below 15: the edge (a, b) is edge FIFO entry X; when Y is 0 then c is `next` and `next` goes up by one, and c is pushed to the vertex FIFO; when Y is from 1 to 12 then c is vertex FIFO entry Y; when Y is 13 then c is `last` minus 1 and when Y is 14 then c is `last` plus 1, and in both cases `last` becomes c and c is pushed to the vertex FIFO; when Y is 15 then c is a read index and c is pushed to the vertex FIFO; then the edges (c, b) and (a, c) are pushed to the edge FIFO, in that order. When X is 15 and Y is below 14: take the code table byte Y with high nibble Z and low nibble W; a is `next` and `next` goes up by one; b is `next` (and `next` goes up) when Z is 0, otherwise vertex FIFO entry Z minus 1; c is `next` (and `next` goes up) when W is 0, otherwise vertex FIFO entry W minus 1 (both entries read before anything new is pushed); then push edges (b, a), (c, b), (a, c) in that order, push a to the vertex FIFO, then b when Z is 0, then c when W is 0. When the code is 0xfe or 0xff: read one more byte from the extra data (false when none is left before the code table), with high nibble Z and low nibble W; when that byte is 0, `next` becomes 0 first; a is `next` (and `next` goes up) for 0xfe and a read index for 0xff; b is `next` (and `next` goes up) when Z is 0, a read index when Z is 15, otherwise vertex FIFO entry Z minus 1; c the same way with W, read after b; then push edges (b, a), (c, b), (a, c), push a to the vertex FIFO, then b when Z is 0 or 15, then c when W is 0 or 15. Every vertex FIFO entry is read from the FIFO as it stood before the triangle's pushes. Each triangle appends a, b and c to out in that order. At the end the extra data must have been read up to exactly the start of the code table, or the result is false.

`indices` takes a stream, a count, an index size and an output byte list, decodes mode 2 and appends count indices of the given size each, giving true when it worked; on any failure it gives false and the output keeps no new bytes. It fails when count is below zero, size is not 2 or 4, the stream is shorter than 5 bytes or its first byte is not 0xd1. Two baselines start at 0. For each index it reads a `varint` (false when that fails or reaches into the last 4 bytes); the number's lowest bit picks the baseline; the rest of the number shifted right by one is zigzag coded: when its lowest bit is set the delta is minus one minus (it shifted right by one), otherwise it shifted right by one. The picked baseline goes up by the delta and is kept to 32 bits as an unsigned number, and the index is that baseline. At the end the reader must stand exactly at the stream's length minus 4.

`s8` and `s16` read the low 8 or 16 bits of a whole number as a signed two's complement number. `rnd` of a real is like JavaScript's Math.round (the floor of the value plus 0.5) as a whole number.

`octahedral` applies filter 1 in place to an output byte list, given a start byte, a count and a stride, and gives false when stride is not 4 or 8. Each element has four components, signed 8-bit for stride 4 and signed 16-bit little-endian for stride 8; let max be 127 or 32767. With one as the third component (false when it is 0): x is the first component over one and y the second over one, as reals; z is 1 minus the absolute value of x minus the absolute value of y; t is the larger of minus z and 0; x goes down by t when x is at least 0 and up by t otherwise, and the same for y; h is max over the square root of x squared plus y squared plus z squared. The first three components become `rnd` of x times h, y times h and z times h, written back as signed numbers of the same width (kept to that many bits); the fourth stays as it was.

`quaternion` applies filter 2 in place the same way and gives false when stride is not 8. Each element has four signed 16-bit little-endian components; w0 is the fourth. m is w0 bitwise-and 3; s is the real number 0.7071067811865476 (the square root of 2, halved) divided by the real value of (w0 bitwise-or 3), with no square root taken of that quotient and no failure for any value of w0; x, y and z are the first three components times s; w is the square root of the larger of 0 and 1 minus x squared minus y squared minus z squared. The components at positions (m plus 1) mod 4, (m plus 2) mod 4, (m plus 3) mod 4 and m become `rnd` of x, y, z and w times 32767, written as signed 16-bit little-endian.

`exponential` applies filter 3 in place the same way and gives false when stride is not a multiple of 4. Every 4-byte little-endian group of the count times stride bytes is a signed 32-bit number v: e is v arithmetic-shifted right by 24 and m is the low 24 bits of v as a signed 24-bit number; the group is replaced by the IEEE single-precision float bits, little-endian, of 2 to the power e times m (computed as a real, then rounded to single precision like C's cast to float).

`color` applies filter 4 in place the same way and gives false when stride is not 4 or 8. Each element has four components, 8-bit for stride 4 and 16-bit little-endian for stride 8; let max be 255 or 65535. y is the first component unsigned, co and cg the second and third as signed numbers, and a0 the fourth unsigned. b is the index of a0's highest set bit (-1 when a0 is 0) and as is 2 to the power (b plus 1), minus 1. r is y plus co minus cg, g is y plus cg, bl is y minus co minus cg; a is a0 bitwise-and (as shifted right by one), then shifted left by one and bitwise-or its own lowest bit. ss is max over as as a real (when as is 0, ss is 0). The four components become `rnd` of r, g, bl and a times ss, kept to the component's width as unsigned numbers.

`decode` takes a stream, a count, a stride, a mode, a filter and an output byte list, appends the decoded view to the output and gives true when it worked. It remembers the output's length first. Mode 0 calls `attributes`, then for filter 1, 2, 3 or 4 the matching filter on the new bytes (starting at the remembered length); filter 0 does nothing more and any other filter fails. Mode 1 calls `triangles` with count and stride as the size, mode 2 calls `indices` the same way, and both fail when filter is not 0. Any other mode fails. Whenever it fails, the output is cut back to the remembered length and the result is false.